# Solution — GIAM 6.6 Problem 5

## Problem

The helix in $ \mathbb{R}^3 $ is

$$$
H \;=\; \{\,(\cos t,\, \sin t,\, t) \;:\; t \in \mathbb{R}\,\}.
$$$

Find $ \pi_{12}(H) $, $ \pi_{13}(H) $, and $ \pi_{23}(H) $, where $ \pi_{ij} : \mathbb{R}^3 \to \mathbb{R}^2 $ keeps the $ i $-th and $ j $-th coordinates and drops the third.

## Setup

The three projections strip away one coordinate:

$$$
\pi_{12}(x, y, z) = (x, y), \qquad \pi_{13}(x, y, z) = (x, z), \qquad \pi_{23}(x, y, z) = (y, z).
$$$

For each $ t \in \mathbb{R} $, the helix passes through $ (\cos t,\, \sin t,\, t) $: the $ (x, y) $-coordinates trace the unit circle while $ z $ rises linearly with $ t $. The projections turn this 3D motion into three 2D "shadows" — one viewed from each coordinate-axis direction.

## $ \pi_{12}(H) $ — the Unit Circle

Apply $ \pi_{12} $ pointwise to $ H $:

$ \pi_{12}\bigl((\cos t,\, \sin t,\, t)\bigr) \;=\; (\cos t,\, \sin t). $

As $ t $ ranges over $ \mathbb{R} $, the point $ (\cos t, \sin t) $ traces the unit circle (each point hit infinitely often, once per rotation). So

$$$
\pi_{12}(H) \;=\; \{\,(\cos t,\, \sin t) \;:\; t \in \mathbb{R}\,\} \;=\; \{\,(x, y) \in \mathbb{R}^2 \;:\; x^2 + y^2 = 1\,\} \;=\; S^1.
$$$

This is the *shadow* of the helix looking straight down the $ z $-axis — the helix's $ xy $-footprint is exactly its underlying circle of revolution.

## $ \pi_{13}(H) $ — the Curve $ x = \cos z $

Apply $ \pi_{13} $:

$ \pi_{13}\bigl((\cos t,\, \sin t,\, t)\bigr) \;=\; (\cos t,\, t). $

Reading the second coordinate as $ z $ (since $ z = t $),

$$$
\pi_{13}(H) \;=\; \{\,(\cos t,\, t) \;:\; t \in \mathbb{R}\,\} \;=\; \{\,(x, z) \in \mathbb{R}^2 \;:\; x = \cos z\,\}.
$$$

That is, the graph of $ x = \cos z $ — a cosine curve "lying on its side," with the height $ z $ on the vertical axis and the horizontal coordinate $ x $ oscillating between $ -1 $ and $ 1 $.

This is the shadow viewed from the $ y $-axis direction: the spiraling helix, seen edge-on, traces a sinusoid in the $ xz $-plane.

## $ \pi_{23}(H) $ — the Curve $ y = \sin z $

Apply $ \pi_{23} $:

$ \pi_{23}\bigl((\cos t,\, \sin t,\, t)\bigr) \;=\; (\sin t,\, t). $

Renaming again,

$$$
\pi_{23}(H) \;=\; \{\,(\sin t,\, t) \;:\; t \in \mathbb{R}\,\} \;=\; \{\,(y, z) \in \mathbb{R}^2 \;:\; y = \sin z\,\}.
$$$

The graph of $ y = \sin z $ — sine curve on its side. The shadow viewed from the $ x $-axis direction.

## Summary

| Projection | Set | Description |
| :---: | :---: | :--- |
| $ \pi_{12}(H) $ | $ \{(\cos t,\, \sin t) : t \in \mathbb{R}\} $ | unit circle $ S^1 $: $ x^2 + y^2 = 1 $ |
| $ \pi_{13}(H) $ | $ \{(\cos t,\, t) : t \in \mathbb{R}\} $ | curve $ x = \cos z $ in the $ xz $-plane |
| $ \pi_{23}(H) $ | $ \{(\sin t,\, t) : t \in \mathbb{R}\} $ | curve $ y = \sin z $ in the $ yz $-plane |

The three shadows together encode the full 3D structure of $ H $: the circular projection captures the rotational sweep around the $ z $-axis, and the two sinusoidal projections capture the linear ascent paired with that rotation.

## Remark — Sine vs. Cosine, 90° Apart

The two side projections, $ \pi_{13}(H) $ and $ \pi_{23}(H) $, are *the same curve up to a $ \pi/2 $ shift in $ z $* — because $ \sin(t) = \cos(t - \pi/2) $.

Geometrically: looking at the helix from the $ y $-axis, you see the $ x $-coordinate of the rotating point as a function of height; looking from the $ x $-axis, you see the $ y $-coordinate, but the rotation is now "$ 90^\circ $ behind." The 90° rotation around the $ z $-axis (between the two viewing directions) is exactly the $ \pi/2 $ phase shift between $ \cos $ and $ \sin $. Two views of the same motion, offset in time.
